- yash-suite-other-hangs: `tests/yash/random-y.tst` (formerly
  `yash-random-y-tst-hangs`) no longer hangs -- confirmed fixed as a
  side effect of unrelated 2026-07-30 work (most likely
  `fixes/111`/`fixes/112`, both about pipeline/redirection-failure
  control flow); it now completes in well under a second and, once
  actually inspected, turned up three genuine `$RANDOM` bugs (seeding,
  non-integer assignment, and `unset` not disabling the magic), fixed
  separately as `fixes/113`.

  However, a full sweep of all 119 `tests/yash/*.tst` files (each run
  individually with a 10s timeout via `run-tst.sh`, same as CTest's own
  per-file `TIMEOUT` but much shorter to survey quickly) found several
  *other* files that still hang or run suspiciously long, so
  `DO_YASH_TESTS` staying off by default (see `TODO.md`) is still
  correct, just for a different, not-yet-isolated reason:

    arith-y.tst, cmdprint-y.tst, pipeline-y.tst, redir-y.tst,
    until-y.tst, while-y.tst -- all hit the 10s timeout (still running,
    not just slow).

    function-y.tst (~8s), if-y.tst (~9.5s), typeset-y.tst (~8.5s) --
    finished but suspiciously slow for a conformance test file; may or
    may not be a real bug (could just be an intentionally slow test
    case), not investigated.

  None of these are isolated to a specific line/case yet -- same next
  step as before applies (debug build + debugger, or bisecting each
  file's test cases individually via a copy with cases removed) but
  hasn't been done for any of them.

- eval-lineno-imprecise-inside-function: `$LINENO` read from within an
  `eval`'d string now counts from the invoking statement's line
  (fixed, see fixes/92) rather than always reporting 1, but the value
  it inherits (`parse_lineno`, stamped once per top-level list by
  `sh_loop()`) is the *call site's* line, not the eval statement's own
  line when eval runs from inside a function or loop body whose
  definition spans multiple lines -- there is no per-command location
  tracking to fall back on for that case (unlike a direct, non-eval'd
  `$LINENO` reference, which reads the parse-time `loc` stamped
  individually on its own `N_ARGPARAM` node). Confirmed:

    f() {
      echo $LINENO   # correctly prints 2
      eval 'echo $LINENO'   # prints 5 (f's call site), should print 3
    }
    f

- set-notify-unimplemented: POSIX `-b` (report a background job's
  completion as soon as it happens, instead of waiting until just
  before the next prompt) is missing the same way -- no `struct
  shopt` bit, not recognized by `builtin_set()`. Belongs in `src/job/`:
  `job_wait.c`/`sh_onsig()`'s SIGCHLD path and `job_clean.c` are where
  a finished background job is currently *noticed*, but
  `job_clean.c`'s own banner-printing comments already describe the
  existing race between the async SIGCHLD path and the synchronous
  `job_wait()` path around exactly this "print immediately vs. defer"
  distinction (a stray banner interrupting a running foreground
  command's own output mid-line is the whole reason it's deferred by
  default) -- so this is more than flipping a condition, it's adding
  a real "OK to interrupt terminal output right now" toggle to logic
  that currently assumes deferral is always correct.

- set-verbose-unimplemented: POSIX `-v` (echo each input line to
  stderr as it's read, before any parsing/expansion touches it --
  distinct from `-x`, which echoes *after* expansion, from
  `src/eval/`'s `E_PRINT` path) is missing the same way. There is
  currently no hook point that sees "a raw input line" as a unit
  separate from `src/source.h`'s character-at-a-time
  `source_peek()`/`source_next()` interface the parser reads through
  -- implementing this needs either a small line-buffering layer at
  that level (echo-and-flush whenever a newline is consumed) or some
  other way to reconstruct "the line just parsed" from `struct
  source`'s position tracking. This is the most structurally invasive
  of this group precisely because nothing else needs "raw source
  text" as such a unit today.

- set-histexpand-unimplemented: `struct shopt`'s `histexpand` bit
  (`src/sh.h`, `-H`) is set correctly by `set -H`/`set +H` and
  reflected back correctly in `$-`, but nothing checks it either --
  more fundamentally, there is no history-expansion ('!') feature
  implemented at all under `src/history/` or `src/term/` for this
  flag to gate. It's a placeholder with nothing behind it.

- (FIXED, fixes/131) tree-h-packed-macro-collides-with-bionic: this
  entry previously claimed the Termux segfault chasing this whole
  investigation was "reproduced under Linux" and merely cosmetic --
  both wrong, corrected here. `src/tree.h` defines its own `__packed`
  macro specifically to be a deliberate no-op:

    #ifndef __packed
    #define __packed /*__attribute__((packed))*/
    #endif

  i.e. every `union node` member struct's `} __packed;` was *meant* to
  compile as an ordinary, naturally-aligned struct -- the real
  `__attribute__((packed))` is commented out. On Android/Termux,
  though, Bionic's own `<sys/cdefs.h>` (transitively included well
  before `tree.h`) already `#define`s `__packed` to the real packed
  attribute -- a completely unrelated, platform-specific macro that
  just happens to share this exact name. `#ifndef __packed` then sees
  Bionic's definition and skips shish's own, so every node struct
  silently *does* end up genuinely packed on that one platform, never
  intentionally, never tested. Different node kinds have different-
  sized fields ahead of their pointer members (some a 1-bit bitfield,
  some a full `unsigned`), so packed offsets don't line up consistently
  across `union node`'s different same-memory views -- a real,
  platform-specific memory-corruption bug, not just pedantic alignment
  UB, though it took a while to tell those apart:

  Never actually reproduced under a plain Linux ASan/UBSan build,
  contrary to what this entry used to claim (the earlier "confirmed
  under Linux" note was never actually checked against this repo's own
  Linux repro logs -- an unforced documentation error, not a real
  cross-platform repro). Verified for real once suspected, by forcing
  the same collision locally via `-D__packed=__attribute__((packed))`
  on a Linux ASan build: this repo's own `./configure` run completed
  clean *without* the forced flag, but crashed reliably *with* it --
  `src/tree/tree_cat.c:34`'s `switch(node->id)` dereferencing a
  corrupted, near-null `node` pointer (`0x72`), reached via exactly the
  packed-layout offset mismatch described above. Confirms the Termux
  crash was this bug the whole time, on Linux the whole time it was
  possible to test for, just never actually tested until asked to look
  again.

  Fixed (`fixes/131`) by renaming shish's own macro from `__packed` to
  `SHISH_TREE_PACKED` throughout `src/tree.h`, so it can no longer
  collide with Bionic's (or any other platform's) same-named macro --
  confirmed clean (zero misalignment reports, zero crash) on a Linux
  ASan/UBSan `./configure` run even with the collision forced via
  `-D__packed=__attribute__((packed))`, i.e. this fix neutralizes the
  exact condition that broke Termux regardless of what that platform's
  own headers define `__packed` to.

- ubsan-buffer-op-proto-function-type-mismatch: same sanitizer sweep as
  the entry above. `lib/buffer.h`'s `buffer_op_proto` is declared as
  `ssize_t(int, void*, size_t, void*)`. Both callbacks shish itself
  actually calls through it at runtime -- `stralloc_write()` (was
  `int(int, const char*, size_t, buffer*)`) and `term_read()` (was
  `int(int, char*, unsigned int)`) -- had incompatible signatures,
  flagged by `-fsanitize=function`:

    lib/buffer/buffer_stubborn.c:19:13: runtime error: call to function
    stralloc_write through pointer to incorrect function type
    'long (*)(int, void *, unsigned long, void *)'
    lib/buffer/buffer_stubborn2.c:18:13: runtime error: call to function
    term_read through pointer to incorrect function type
    'long (*)(int, void *, unsigned long, void *)'

  Fixed by changing both functions' own signatures to match
  `buffer_op_proto` exactly (casting the generic `void*`/`size_t`
  parameters back to their real types inside the function body
  instead of casting the function pointer at the call site) -- this
  removes every `(buffer_op_proto*)(void*)&...` cast that fed either
  of them, confirmed clean (zero `-fsanitize=function` reports) across
  a full `./configure` run under the ASan/UBSan build.

  `read`/`write` (external libc, `int(int,void*,size_t)`) are also
  cast to `buffer_op_proto*` throughout `src/fd/`, `src/fdtable/`,
  `src/history/`, and `src/sh/`, but never trigger this check:
  `-fsanitize=function` embeds its type-descriptor comparison only in
  functions the sanitizer itself compiled, and libc's `read`/`write`
  weren't -- so calling through the mismatched pointer type is still
  UB by the letter of the standard, just not one this tooling can ever
  actually observe. Not touched, since "fix" here would mean wrapping
  two libc functions everywhere they're used for a change with no
  observable effect under any sanitizer or real ABI this project
  targets.

  Two more real mismatches remain, in `lib/buffer/buffer_tosa.c`
  (`strallocwrite`, `char*` where `buffer_op_proto` wants `void*`) and
  `lib/buffer/buffer_frombuf.c` (`buffer_dummyreadbuf`, `fd_t` where
  it wants `int` -- identical on every platform this project builds
  for, but not the same declared type) -- both dead code from shish's
  own perspective (`buffer_tosa()`/`buffer_frombuf()`, the functions
  that would ever install them as a callback, are never called
  anywhere under `src/`; they only exist because `lib/`'s whole-
  directory glob build compiles every libowfat source file, used or
  not). Left alone rather than "fixed" per this repo's own standing
  preference for not touching unused `lib/` code unasked.

- asan-leak-residue-not-fully-triaged: requested directly (fix ASan
  leak reports from a full `./configure` run under the same
  `-fsanitize=address,undefined` build used for the entries above).
  Found and fixed the two largest, confirmed-real leaks:

  `lib/stralloc/stralloc_trunc.c` (`fixes/129`) never updated `sa->a`
  (the allocated-capacity field `stralloc_free()`/`stralloc_ready()`
  rely on to know whether `sa->s` owns real heap storage) after
  `alloc_re()`ing a bigger buffer -- so a stralloc that had just been
  `stralloc_init()`'d (`a` freshly 0) and then grown via
  `stralloc_trunc()` looked "unallocated" to any later
  `stralloc_free()` on it, silently leaking the real buffer instead of
  freeing it. `var_setvsa()` hits exactly this sequence once per
  `for`-loop iteration (`eval_for.c`) for the loop variable -- minimal
  repro: `for x in a b c d e f g h i j; do :; done` leaked 9 objects
  (10 iterations, first one didn't yet have a stale `a` to trip over).
  Fixed at the shared primitive, not the call site, since any other
  `stralloc_trunc()` caller relying on `.a` staying accurate afterward
  (`path_readlink.c` also calls it) had the identical latent bug.

  `src/parse/parse_simple_command.c` (`fixes/130`): a local `stralloc
  sa` used purely as scratch space to pass a nul/length-safe string to
  `parse_findalias()` was never `stralloc_free()`'d, leaking on every
  simple command whose first word is a literal string (i.e. most
  commands in most scripts).

  Together these accounted for most of a full gettext-tools-style
  `./configure` run's leak volume (measured before/after on this
  repo's own, much shorter `./configure`: 304073 bytes/6941
  allocations down to 234452 bytes/4780 allocations across the whole
  run's forked subshells). What's left, not yet individually
  triaged:

  - A large, expected residue is `struct var` entries
    (`src/var/var_create.c`) and their backing storage -- the variable
    table is deliberately process-lifetime state (like any shell's),
    reclaimed by the OS at exit rather than explicitly freed, so
    LeakSanitizer necessarily reports it as "leaked" the same way it
    would for any long-lived global root; not a bug, not planned to be
    "fixed" by adding an exit-time teardown pass whose only purpose
    would be silencing the sanitizer.
  - `sh_loop()`'s own `cmd`/`p.sa` scratch stralloc's (reused, not
    freed, once per process for the same reason) are the same
    category.
  - Smaller, not-yet-investigated groups remain via
    `redir_parse.c:108`, `expand_cat.c:77`, `expand_args.c:67`, and
    `eval_function.c:57` (each `tree_newnode()`/`alloc()`-backed,
    roughly 20-25 objects per full run) -- plausibly real per-node
    leaks in the same family as the two fixed above (something built
    during parsing/expansion never reachable from the tree that
    eventually gets `tree_free()`'d), but not yet root-caused to a
    specific missing free the way the two fixed ones were. Good next
    target if picking this up again: reproduce each with a minimal
    script the way the `for`-loop leak above was isolated, rather than
    working from the full `./configure` run's noise.
